WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.270
At the market this morning, a mango

2
00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.810
seller judged a mango in two seconds.

3
00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:07.890
One squeeze, one look at its

4
00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.710
colour: “Eat it tomorrow.”

5
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.820
She’s handled thousands, so she compares.

6
00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.530
Can a computer do that?

7
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.380
Today: how a classifier decides,

8
00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.140
by asking its neighbours.

9
00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.340
A computer can’t squeeze a mango, so

10
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.720
we describe it with numbers.

11
00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.690
Softness, from zero to ten.

12
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.810
Colour, from green at zero

13
00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:34.570
to yellow at ten.

14
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.090
Measurable numbers like these

15
00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:38.210
are called features.

16
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.680
Now put every mango you’ve checked

17
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.980
on a chart: softness along the

18
00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.880
bottom, colour up the side.

19
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:49.630
Each is a dot, marked ripe or not.

20
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.860
Ripe ones gather in one

21
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.880
corner, unripe in the other.

22
00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.710
A new mango arrives, with no label.

23
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.080
Find the three closest dots:

24
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.520
its nearest neighbours.

25
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.560
Two are ripe and one isn’t, so

26
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.960
the majority says ripe.

27
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.480
No rules, just: which

28
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.060
mangoes is it most like?

29
00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.920
A classifier is a model that puts a new

30
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.460
example into a class, like ripe or not

31
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:17.700
ripe.

32
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.240
Nearest neighbours is one simple kind: it

33
00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.240
compares the new example with stored

34
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:24.880
ones, and goes with the majority.

35
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.130
It’s like a grandmother who can

36
00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:29.410
tell when it will rain.

37
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.820
She remembers days that felt like today:

38
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.360
the same heavy air, the same dark clouds.

39
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.390
Most of them ended in rain, so

40
00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:40.710
she takes the washing in.

41
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.100
Her nearest neighbours are the

42
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.760
days most like today!

43
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:46.510
Quick check!

44
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.060
A new mango’s five nearest

45
00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.160
neighbours: three ripe, two not.

46
00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:56.090
What does the classifier say?

47
00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.430
Ripe: three of its five

48
00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.510
neighbours are ripe.

49
00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.600
But the number you ask, called k, matters.

50
00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:08.590
Ask only one, and a single odd or

51
00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.510
mislabelled mango decides.

52
00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.470
Ask too many, and faraway

53
00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.130
mangoes outvote close ones.

54
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.500
In between usually works best.

55
00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.150
Features matter as much as neighbours.

56
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.880
Softness and colour separate ripe from

57
00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.420
unripe, so close neighbours agree.

58
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.200
Weight alone doesn’t: a big unripe

59
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.840
mango sits right beside a big ripe

60
00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.080
one, and its neighbours mislead.

61
00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:39.270
In the app, mangoes sit on a

62
00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.610
chart: filled circles are ready to

63
00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.010
eat, open squares not yet.

64
00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.040
Tap to place a new mango, then

65
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.260
slide k from one to fifteen and

66
00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.400
watch the answer change.

67
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.880
Then try the data with mistakes.

68
00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:56.130
Your turn.

69
00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.070
Your model must tell

70
00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.030
handwritten sevens from ones.

71
00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.260
Which feature helps more?

72
00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:06.850
How wide the digit is: a seven is

73
00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.010
wider than a one, whatever the ink.

74
00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.890
Remember: describe each example

75
00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.570
with features you can measure.

76
00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.680
A new example takes the class of most

77
00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.280
of its k nearest neighbours.

78
00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.720
And choose features that separate classes.

79
00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:26.120
Try it yourself: score ten fruits for

80
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.920
size and softness, from zero to ten.

81
00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:31.020
Mark each on a chart,

82
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.140
labelled ripe or not.

83
00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.230
Then score a new fruit, and let its

84
00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:37.990
three nearest neighbours decide.
